  "summary": "Pichhwai (also pichwai, pichhavai, pichhvai, pechhavai etc), literally meaning 'that which hangs from the back' from the Sanskrit words \"Pichh\" means back and \"wais\" means hanging, are large devotional Hindu painted pictures, normally on cloth, which portray Krishna.",
  "description": "Pichhwai (also pichwai, pichhavai, pichhvai, pechhavai etc), literally meaning 'that which hangs from the back' from the Sanskrit words \"Pichh\" means back and \"wais\" means hanging, are large devotional Hindu painted pictures, normally on cloth, which portray Krishna. They are  mainly made to hang in Hindu temples of the Pushtimarg devotional tradition, especially the Shrinathji Temple in Nathdwara, Rajasthan, built around 1672. They are hung behind the idol of Shrinathji, a local form of Krishna and the centre of Pushtimarg worship, to depict his leelas.  Aurangabad was another area associated with them.  The purpose of pichhwais, other than artistic appeal, is to narrate tales of Krishna to the illiterate. Temples have sets with different images, which are changed according to the calendar of festivals celebrating the deity.\n\nNathdwara painting covers these and similar works in other genres, especially Indian miniature paintings.  Like the Pushtimarg tradition, they originated in the Deccan, but are now mainly associated with Western India.",
